### The Architecture
1.  **Local First:** All data is saved to the browser's IndexedDB immediately.
2.  **Sync Logic:** An event listener checks for `online` status to push queued data to your server.

---

### 1. The Setup (HTML)
```html
<form id="survey-form">
  <input type="text" id="site-name" placeholder="Site Name" required>
  <button type="submit">Save Survey</button>
</form>
<div id="status">Status: Online</div>
```

### 2. The Logic (JavaScript)
We will use the [idb](https://github.com/jakearchibald/idb) library to make IndexedDB easy to use.

```javascript
import { openDB } from 'idb';

const dbPromise = openDB('survey-db', 1, {
  upgrade(db) { db.createObjectStore('surveys', { keyPath: 'id', autoIncrement: true }); }
});

// 1. Save to Local DB
document.getElementById('survey-form').addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const data = { name: document.getElementById('site-name').value, synced: false };

  const db = await dbPromise;
  await db.add('surveys', data);
  console.log("Saved locally");
  syncData(); // Attempt sync
});

// 2. Sync Logic
async function syncData() {
  if (!navigator.onLine) return;

  const db = await dbPromise;
  const tx = db.transaction('surveys', 'readwrite');
  const store = tx.objectStore('surveys');
  const items = await store.getAll();

  for (const item of items) {
    if (!item.synced) {
      try {
        await fetch('/api/surveys', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(item) });
        item.synced = true;
        await store.put(item); // Update status
      } catch (err) { console.error("Sync failed", err); }
    }
  }
}

// 3. Listen for reconnection
window.addEventListener('online', syncData);
```

---

### Key Components Explained

#### A. IndexedDB (The "Offline" Storage)
Unlike `localStorage`, IndexedDB is asynchronous and can handle large amounts of survey data (photos, coordinates, etc.). By using the `idb` wrapper, you can use `await` syntax to keep your code clean.

#### B. The Sync Flag
Notice the `synced: false` property. This is crucial for offline-first apps. It allows you to distinguish between data that needs to be sent and data that is already safely on your server.

#### C. The `online` Event
The browser automatically fires the `online` event when the network connection returns. By calling `syncData()` inside this listener, the app "wakes up" and empties its local queue automatically without user intervention.

