This example features a dashboard that updates a live chart when you submit a new data point via a form.

### The Code (`index.html`)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Real-Time Dashboard</title>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js"></script>
    <style>
        body { font-family: sans-serif; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; padding: 20px; }
        .container { width: 80%; max-width: 800px; }
        form { margin-bottom: 20px; padding: 20px; background: #f4f4f4; border-radius: 8px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="container">
        <h2>Live Data Dashboard</h2>

        <form id="dataForm">
            <input type="number" id="valInput" placeholder="Enter value" required>
            <button type="submit">Add to Chart</button>
        </form>

        <canvas id="myChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <script>
        // 1. Initialize Chart
        const ctx = document.getElementById('myChart').getContext('2d');
        const myChart = new Chart(ctx, {
            type: 'line',
            data: {
                labels: ['Start'],
                datasets: [{
                    label: 'Live Metrics',
                    data: [0],
                    borderColor: 'rgb(75, 192, 192)',
                    tension: 0.1
                }]
            }
        });

        // 2. Handle Form Submission
        const form = document.getElementById('dataForm');
        form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
            e.preventDefault();
            const val = document.getElementById('valInput').value;

            // Add new data point
            const time = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();
            myChart.data.labels.push(time);
            myChart.data.datasets[0].data.push(val);

            // Keep only last 10 points
            if (myChart.data.labels.length > 10) {
                myChart.data.labels.shift();
                myChart.data.datasets[0].data.shift();
            }

            myChart.update(); // Refresh chart
            form.reset();
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:

1.  **Chart.js Integration**: We load the library via CDN. The `canvas` element is the anchor point for the graph.
2.  **Form Handling**: We prevent the default browser refresh (`e.preventDefault()`) when the button is clicked. We capture the input value and push it into the Chart.js data object.
3.  **Real-Time Updates**: 
    *   `myChart.update()` is called every time the form is submitted to re-render the canvas.
    *   We include a simple logic (`.shift()`) to keep the chart clean by removing old data points once the array exceeds 10 items.

### How to make this "Production Ready":

*   **For WebSocket Data**: If you want the chart to update automatically without a form, replace the form logic with a WebSocket listener:
    ```javascript
    const socket = new WebSocket('ws://your-data-source.com');
    socket.onmessage = (event) => {
        const newData = JSON.parse(event.data);
        // Add logic to push newData to chart and call myChart.update()
    };
    ```
*   **For State Management**: If the dashboard becomes complex (many charts/forms), consider using **React** with **Recharts** or **Vue** with **vue-chartjs**.
*   **Styling**: Use a framework like **Tailwind CSS** to quickly make the dashboard responsive and professional.

