Save this code as an `.html` file and open it in any browser.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Minimal Dashboard</title>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js"></script>
</head>
<body>

    <div style="width: 500px;">
        <canvas id="myChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <form id="dataForm" style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <input type="number" id="val" placeholder="Enter value" required>
        <button type="submit">Add Data</button>
    </form>

    <script>
        const ctx = document.getElementById('myChart').getContext('2d');
        const chart = new Chart(ctx, {
            type: 'bar',
            data: {
                labels: ['Initial'],
                datasets: [{ label: 'My Data', data: [10] }]
            }
        });

        document.getElementById('dataForm').addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
            e.preventDefault();
            const val = document.getElementById('val').value;

            // Update Chart
            chart.data.labels.push('New');
            chart.data.datasets[0].data.push(val);
            chart.update();

            e.target.reset();
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:
1.  **CDN Integration:** We pull `Chart.js` directly from a CDN, avoiding the need for `npm` or complex build tools.
2.  **The Canvas:** The `<canvas>` element is the target where the library renders the graphics.
3.  **The Chart Instance:** We initialize the chart with an initial label and value.
4.  **Form Handling:** We listen for the `submit` event. `e.preventDefault()` stops the page from refreshing. We then push the new value into the chart's data array and call `chart.update()` to trigger the animation.

### Scaling this up:
If you want to move beyond this minimal version, consider these steps:

