To create a minimal, functional version of a real-time drag-and-drop board, we will use:
1.  **HTML5 Drag and Drop API** (Native, no heavy libraries).
2.  **Firebase Realtime Database** (The easiest way to handle "real-time" without building a custom backend/WebSockets).

### 1. The Setup
Create an `index.html` file. You will need to replace `YOUR_FIREBASE_CONFIG` with your own project config from the [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        .board { display: flex; gap: 20px; padding: 20px; }
        .column { background: #eee; width: 200px; padding: 10px; min-height: 300px; }
        .task { background: white; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; cursor: grab; border: 1px solid #ccc; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="board">
    <div class="column" id="todo" ondrop="drop(event)" ondragover="allowDrop(event)">
        <h3>To Do</h3>
        <div id="tasks-todo"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="column" id="done" ondrop="drop(event)" ondragover="allowDrop(event)">
        <h3>Done</h3>
        <div id="tasks-done"></div>
    </div>
</div>

<script type="module">
    import { initializeApp } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.6.1/firebase-app.js";
    import { getDatabase, ref, onValue, update } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.6.1/firebase-database.js";

    const firebaseConfig = { /* YOUR FIREBASE CONFIG HERE */ };
    const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
    const db = getDatabase(app);

    // Render tasks from Firebase
    onValue(ref(db, 'tasks'), (snapshot) => {
        const tasks = snapshot.val() || {};
        document.getElementById('tasks-todo').innerHTML = '';
        document.getElementById('tasks-done').innerHTML = '';

        for (let id in tasks) {
            const task = tasks[id];
            const el = document.createElement('div');
            el.className = 'task';
            el.draggable = true;
            el.id = id;
            el.innerText = task.text;
            el.ondragstart = (e) => e.dataTransfer.setData("text", id);
            document.getElementById(`tasks-${task.status}`).appendChild(el);
        }
    });

    window.allowDrop = (e) => e.preventDefault();
    window.drop = (e) => {
        const taskId = e.dataTransfer.getData("text");
        const newStatus = e.target.closest('.column').id;
        update(ref(db, 'tasks/' + taskId), { status: newStatus });
    };
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 2. How to make it work
1.  **Firebase Project:** Go to [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/), create a project, and enable **Realtime Database**.
2.  **Database Rules:** Set your rules to `read/write: true` for testing (Note: Secure these before production).
3.  **Initial Data:** Manually add data to your Firebase Realtime Database to see it appear:
    ```json
    {
