To create a minimal, functional project management board with drag-and-drop and real-time collaboration, we will use:

1.  **HTML5 Drag and Drop API** (Native, no heavy libraries).
2.  **Firebase Realtime Database** (The easiest way to handle "real-time" without setting up a backend server).

### 1. Setup
Create a file named `index.html`. You will need to replace `YOUR_FIREBASE_CONFIG` with your actual Firebase project config (from the Firebase Console).

### 2. The Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Minimal Kanban</title>
    <style>
        .board { display: flex; gap: 20px; padding: 20px; }
        .column { background: #f4f4f4; width: 200px; padding: 10px; min-height: 300px; }
        .task { background: white; padding: 10px; margin: 5px 0; cursor: grab; border: 1px solid #ccc; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="board">
    <div class="column" id="todo" ondrop="drop(event)" ondragover="allowDrop(event)"><h3>To Do</h3></div>
    <div class="column" id="done" ondrop="drop(event)" ondragover="allowDrop(event)"><h3>Done</h3></div>
</div>

<input id="taskInput" placeholder="New task...">
<button onclick="addTask()">Add Task</button>

<!-- Firebase SDKs -->
<script type="module">
    import { initializeApp } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.6.1/firebase-app.js";
    import { getDatabase, ref, push, onValue, update } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.6.1/firebase-database.js";

    const firebaseConfig = { /* YOUR FIREBASE CONFIG HERE */ };
    const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
    const db = getDatabase(app);

    // Add Task
    window.addTask = () => {
        const text = document.getElementById('taskInput').value;
        push(ref(db, 'tasks'), { text, status: 'todo' });
    };

    // Drag and Drop Logic
    window.allowDrop = (e) => e.preventDefault();
    window.drag = (e) => e.dataTransfer.setData("text", e.target.id);
    window.drop = (e) => {
        const id = e.dataTransfer.getData("text");
        const newStatus = e.target.closest('.column').id;
        update(ref(db, `tasks/${id}`), { status: newStatus });
    };

    // Real-time Sync
    onValue(ref(db, 'tasks'), (snapshot) => {
        const data = snapshot.val() || {};
        document.querySelectorAll('.column').forEach(c => c.innerHTML = `<h3>${c.querySelector('h3').innerText}</h3>`);

        for (let id in data) {
            const task = data[id];
            const el = document.createElement('div');
            el.className = 'task';
            el.id = id;
            el.draggable = true;
            el.innerText = task.text;
            el.ondragstart = window.drag;
            document.getElementById(task.status).appendChild(el);
        }
    });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:

1.  **Real-time sync:** The `onValue` listener from Firebase acts as a "live stream." Whenever any user adds a task or drags one, the database updates, and every connected browser automatically re-renders the board.
2.  **Drag and Drop:** 
    *   `ondragstart` stores the unique task ID in the browser's data transfer object.
    *   `ondrop` triggers a Firebase `update()` command, changing the `status` field of that specific task in the database.
3.  **Minimalism:** There is no complex state management or heavy frameworks (React/Vue). The DOM is simply cleared and rebuilt whenever the database changes.

### Steps to run:
1.  Go to [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/).
2.  Create a project and add a **Web App**.
3.  Copy the `firebaseConfig` object into the script above.
4.  In Firebase, go to **Realtime Database**, create a database, and set the Rules to:
    ```json
