### The Code (`index.html`)

You can save this as an `.html` file and open it in any browser.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; }</style>
</head>
<body>
    <script type="module">
        import * as THREE from 'https://unpkg.com/three@0.158.0/build/three.module.js';

        // 1. Setup Scene
        const scene = new THREE.Scene();
        const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth/window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
        const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
        renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
        document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

        // 2. Dynamic Lighting
        const light = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1);
        light.position.set(5, 5, 5);
        scene.add(light);
        scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0x404040));

        // 3. Custom Shader Material
        const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
            uniforms: { time: { value: 0 } },
            vertexShader: `
                varying vec2 vUv;
                void main() {
                    vUv = uv;
                    gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.0);
                }
            `,
            fragmentShader: `
                uniform float time;
                varying vec2 vUv;
                void main() {
                    // Dynamic color pulse effect
                    float pulse = abs(sin(time * 2.0));
                    gl_FragColor = vec4(vUv.x * pulse, vUv.y, 0.5 + 0.5 * pulse, 1.0);
                }
            `
        });

        const cube = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1), material);
        scene.add(cube);
        camera.position.z = 3;

        // 4. Animation Loop
        function animate(t) {
            requestAnimationFrame(animate);
            cube.rotation.x += 0.01;
            cube.rotation.y += 0.01;
            material.uniforms.time.value = t * 0.001;
            renderer.render(scene, camera);
        }
        animate();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Components Explained:

1.  **The Renderer:** `WebGLRenderer` converts your 3D objects into pixels on the `<canvas>` element.
2.  **The Scene Graph:** The `scene` object acts as a container for all your objects, lights, and cameras.
3.  **Dynamic Lighting:** We added a `DirectionalLight` to simulate a sun-like source. The `ShaderMaterial` reacts to this light (or in this case, uses its own logic to calculate color).
4.  **Shaders:**
    *   **Vertex Shader:** Runs on every corner (vertex) of the cube to determine its position on the screen.
    *   **Fragment Shader:** Runs on every pixel. We use the `time` uniform to oscillate the color values, creating the "dynamic" effect.
5.  **The Loop:** `requestAnimationFrame` creates the real-time render loop, which typically runs at 60 frames per second.

### How to expand this:
*   **Add Models:** Use `GLTFLoader` to import `.glb` files (Blender exports).
*   **Interactivity:** Add `OrbitControls` (from the Three.js examples folder) to allow the user to click and drag to rotate the view.
*   **Post-processing:** Add `EffectComposer` to add bloom, depth of field, or color grading.

To create a minimal 3D viewer, we will use **Three.js**, the industry-standard library for WebGL. This example includes a 3D object, dynamic lighting, and a custom shader effect (a pulsing glow).

### The Minimal Implementation

You can save this code as an `.html` file and open it in any browser.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>
    <script type="module">
        import * as THREE from 'https://cdn.skypack.dev/three@0.136.0';

        // 1. Setup Scene
        const scene = new THREE.Scene();
        const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth/window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
        const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
        renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
        document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

        // 2. Add Dynamic Lighting
        const light = new THREE.PointLight(0xffffff, 1, 100);
        light.position.set(5, 5, 5);
        scene.add(light);
        scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0x404040));

        // 3. Shader Material (The "Effect")
        const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
            uniforms: { time: { value: 0 } },
            vertexShader: `
                varying vec3 vNormal;
                void main() {
                    vNormal = normal;
                    gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.0);
                }`,
            fragmentShader: `
                uniform float time;
                varying vec3 vNormal;
                void main() {
                    float intensity = pow(0.7 - dot(vNormal, vec3(0,0,1)), 2.0);
                    gl_FragColor = vec4(0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.0) * intensity * (sin(time) + 2.0);
                }`
        });

        const cube = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1), material);
        scene.add(cube);
        camera.position.z = 3;

        // 4. Animation Loop
        function animate(t) {
            requestAnimationFrame(animate);
            cube.rotation.x += 0.01;
            cube.rotation.y += 0.01;
            material.uniforms.time.value = t * 0.005;
            renderer.render(scene, camera);
        }
        animate();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:

1.  **The Scene (`THREE.Scene`)**: Acts as the container for all objects, cameras, and lights.
2.  **The Renderer**: Uses WebGL to draw the mathematical scene onto an HTML `<canvas>`.
3.  **The Shader (`ShaderMaterial`)**:
    *   **Vertex Shader**: Runs once per vertex. It transforms the 3D coordinates into 2D screen space.
    *   **Fragment Shader**: Runs once per pixel. We use the `dot` product of the surface normal and the camera view to create a "Fresnel" (glow) effect, which pulses based on the `time` uniform.
4.  **The Loop (`requestAnimationFrame`)**: This is the heartbeat of the application. It updates the rotation and time values 60 times per second to create the "real-time" effect.

### Next Steps to Expand
